{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a prominent figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century. Born around 1130, he succeeded his father, Bertrand, as the Count of Tripoli in 1142. During his reign, Raymond III focused on consolidating his power and expanding his territories, which included the County of Tripoli, part of the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 1* - **Reign and consolidation of power**:\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by his efforts to strengthen the County of Tripoli. He faced numerous challenges, including the ambitions of the King of Jerusalem, Baldwin III, who sought to assert control over the County of Tripoli. To counter this, Raymond III formed alliances with other Crusader states and local Muslim leaders, aiming to maintain his independence and expand his influence. His strategic marriages, such as his union with Hodierna of Jerusalem, also helped him secure his position.\n\n*highlighted section part 2* - **Role in the Second Crusade**:\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Second Crusade, which began in 1147. He initially supported the Crusade but later had conflicts with the leaders, particularly King Louis VII of France and King Conrad III of Germany. These disputes were partly due to Raymond III's focus on his own interests and the desire to maintain his autonomy. Despite these tensions, he participated in the Siege of Damascus in 1148, a pivotal event in the Crusade.\n\n*highlighted section part 3* - **Later life and legacy**:\nAfter the Second Crusade, Raymond III continued to rule Tripoli, facing ongoing threats from the Muslim leader Nur ad-Din. In 1152, Raymond III died, leaving his County of Tripoli to his son, Raymond IV. His reign is remembered for his efforts to maintain the independence of Tripoli amidst the complex political landscape of the Levant, as well as his contributions to 